{% block article_body %}
<section aria-labelledby="bind"><h2 id="bind">Bind and size the workers</h2>
<p><code>WireframeServer</code> is a typestate. <code>new</code> returns an unbound server; <code>bind</code> or <code>bind_existing_listener</code> returns a bound one, which can report <code>local_addr</code> and run. Configuration that belongs before binding, such as workers, backoff, readiness, and the preamble, is set on either.</p>
{{ code.code('tests/production.rs', 'server', title='production.rs · configure, bind, run, stop') }}
<ul>
  <li><strong>Workers.</strong> One accept-loop task per worker, defaulting to the number of CPU cores and never fewer than one.</li>
  <li><strong>Applications.</strong> In {{ wireframe_version }} the factory is called once for every accepted connection, so per-connection state can live in the application. 0.4.0 changes this; see <a href="/wireframe/roadmap/">the roadmap</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Accept backoff.</strong> When <code>accept</code> fails, as it does when the process runs out of file descriptors, a worker waits and retries with exponential backoff between <code>initial_delay</code> and <code>max_delay</code>. Out-of-range values are corrected, not rejected.</li>
  <li><strong>Panics.</strong> A panic inside a connection task is caught, logged, and counted; the worker and every other connection carry on.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="shutdown"><h2 id="shutdown">Readiness and shutdown</h2>
<p><code>ready_signal</code> takes a oneshot sender that fires once the workers are accepting, which is what a test or an orchestration probe should wait for. <code>run</code> serves until Ctrl+C; <code>run_with_shutdown</code> serves until the future it is given completes, then stops the workers. The sample stops the server through a oneshot channel and checks that it returns cleanly.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="limits"><h2 id="limits">Timeouts and failure limits</h2>
<div class="wf-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Server limits in wireframe 0.3.0"><table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Setting</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">Effect in {{ wireframe_version }}</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>buffer_capacity</code></td><td>1,024 bytes</td><td>Frames over the limit are a decoder error, and the connection closes</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>read_timeout_ms</code></td><td>100&nbsp;ms</td><td>A housekeeping interval: when it elapses with no frame, expired partial messages are purged and the connection keeps waiting</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deserialization failures</td><td>10 in a row</td><td>The connection closes; any good frame resets the count</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>preamble_timeout</code></td><td>None</td><td>A preamble that does not arrive in time goes to the failure handler, and the connection closes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Memory budgets</td><td>16, 64, 64 &times; frame limit</td><td>Reads slow at 80% of the aggregate cap and the connection closes past it; see <a href="/wireframe/guides/large-messages/#budgets">memory budgets</a></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
{% call ui.callout('fact', 'An idle connection is never closed by the read timeout') %}<p>Despite its name, <code>read_timeout_ms</code> does not disconnect a quiet peer. In {{ wireframe_version }} an idle connection stays open until the peer closes it or the server shuts down. Bound idle time at the protocol level, for example with a heartbeat the server expects, or in front of the server.</p>{% endcall %}
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="metrics"><h2 id="metrics">Metrics</h2>
<p>With the default <code>metrics</code> feature, wireframe records through the <a href="https://crates.io/crates/metrics"><code>metrics</code></a> crate, so any recorder installed by the application receives them. With the feature off, the recording helpers become no-ops, so instrumentation can stay in place. The names are public constants:</p>
{{ code.code('tests/production.rs', 'metrics', title='production.rs · metric names') }}
<div class="wf-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Metrics wireframe records"><table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Name</th><th scope="col">Kind</th><th scope="col">Records</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>wireframe_connections_active</code></td><td>Gauge</td><td>Open connections</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>wireframe_frames_processed_total</code></td><td>Counter</td><td>Frames, labelled by direction</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>wireframe_errors_total</code></td><td>Counter</td><td>Errors, labelled by kind</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>wireframe_connection_panics_total</code></td><td>Counter</td><td>Panicking connection tasks</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>wireframe_codec_errors_total</code></td><td>Counter</td><td>Codec errors, labelled by type and recovery policy</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Logging goes through both <code>log</code> and <code>tracing</code>. Install a subscriber or logger to see the warnings for unrouted messages, rejected frames, and preamble errors.</p>
</section>
{% endblock %}
